---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';

const title = 'Free Fake Data Generator - Test Data: Names, Emails, CSV, JSON, SQL';
const description =
  'Generate unlimited fake test data in your browser: names, emails, phones, addresses, companies, dates, UUIDs. Export CSV, JSON, or SQL INSERTs. Seeded and reproducible. No signup, nothing uploaded.';
const h1 = 'Fake data generator';
const lede =
  'Unlimited realistic test data: names, emails, addresses, companies, dates, UUIDs. Export CSV, JSON, or SQL. Seeded, so the same seed always reproduces the same rows.';
---
<ToolPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  h1={h1}
  lede={lede}
  crumbs={[{ label: 'Fake data generator', href: '/fake-data-generator' }]}
  toolName="FreeKit Fake Data Generator"
  intro={[
    'Mockaroo is the name most developers reach for when they need test data, and its free tier stops at 1,000 rows; anything serious starts at $50 a year. That pricing made sense when generating data required a server. It does not anymore. Random names, addresses, and dates are a few kilobytes of word lists plus a seeded random number generator, which is exactly what this page ships, running entirely on your machine.',
    'Pick your columns from 21 types: first and last names, full names, emails, phone numbers, street addresses, cities, states, ZIP codes, countries, companies, job titles, lorem text, integers, decimals, dates, UUIDs, and yes/no flags. Set the row count anywhere from 10 to 10,000, choose CSV, JSON, or SQL INSERTs, and download the file. Every run is seeded, and the seed is shown on screen, so the exact same dataset can be reproduced whenever a test needs to run twice.',
    'Two honest notes. First, everything runs in your browser and nothing is uploaded; your column setup is even remembered in this browser for next time. Second, the data is obviously fake: plausible-looking rows for development and testing, not real people, and not for fraud. Emails use reserved domains like example.com that can never reach a real inbox, and phone numbers use the fictional 555 exchange, on purpose.',
  ]}
  steps={[
    'The page starts with a sensible preset: name, email, phone, address, company, job title, and a signup date. Use the add control to append any of the 21 column types.',
    'Click a column\u2019s label to rename it. That label becomes the CSV header, the JSON key, or the SQL column name.',
    'Tune each column: word counts for lorem text, min and max for integers and decimals, and from/to dates for the date column.',
    'Choose 10 to 10,000 rows and pick CSV, JSON, or SQL INSERTs. For SQL, set the table name too.',
    'Note the seed, then download. The same seed with the same settings always reproduces the same file, which is what you want for repeatable tests. Hit re-roll for a fresh dataset.',
  ]}
  specs={[
    ['Cost', 'Free, unlimited files within the per-file row cap'],
    ['Signup', 'None. No account, no email.'],
    ['Column types', '21: names, email, phone, addresses, company, job title, lorem text, integers, decimals, dates, UUIDs, yes/no'],
    ['Rows per file', '10 to 10,000'],
    ['Output formats', 'CSV (RFC 4180 quoting), pretty JSON, SQL INSERTs batched 500 rows per statement'],
    ['Reproducibility', 'Seeded RNG. The seed is shown, editable, and re-rollable; same seed and settings give the same file'],
    ['Safety', 'Data is obviously fake and generated locally. Not for fraud.'],
    ['Where data is generated', 'In your browser, on your device. Nothing is uploaded.'],
    ['Settings memory', 'Your column setup is remembered in this browser'],
    ['Works offline', 'Yes, once the page has loaded'],
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Is the fake data really unlimited?',
      a: 'Within the per-file cap of 10,000 rows, yes, and you can generate as many files as you like. There is no account counting your downloads and no paid tier above the free one, because the generation happens on your own device and costs nobody anything. If one file is not enough rows, generate several with different seeds.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What does the seed do?',
      a: 'The seed starts the random number generator, so the same seed with the same columns and row count always produces the byte-identical file. That matters for testing: a failing test can be rerun against the exact same data, and a seed shared with a teammate reproduces your dataset on their machine. Change the seed with the re-roll button for a fresh dataset.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is the data realistic enough for demos and testing?',
      a: 'It looks right at a glance: real first and last names, plausible street addresses, properly formatted emails, dates, and UUIDs. It is random combinations, though, not a real population, so use it for UI demos, form testing, database seeding, and load tests. Do not use it where the statistical shape of real data matters.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why do the emails use example.com?',
      a: 'Because example.com, mail.test, sample.org, and the other domains used here are reserved specifically so they can never belong to a real person. Test data has a way of escaping into real systems, and a signup email accidentally sent to a generated address should bounce harmlessly, not land in a stranger\u2019s inbox. Phone numbers use the fictional 555 exchange for the same reason.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can I use this for load testing?',
      a: 'Yes. Ten thousand rows per file in CSV or SQL INSERTs is a solid chunk for seeding a test database or hammering an import endpoint, and multiple files with different seeds stack without duplicates getting in the way conceptually. For very large volumes, generate several files and concatenate them; each file is plain text.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is my generated data private?',
      a: 'Completely. Generation happens in your browser with a local word list and a local random number generator, so there is nothing to intercept and no server that ever sees your column choices. The data itself is fake by design, but the privacy property still matters when your test schema mirrors a real one.',
    },
    {
      q: 'How is this different from Mockaroo?',
      a: 'Mockaroo offers more field types, an API, and larger datasets on paid plans starting around $50 a year with 1,000 free rows. This tool covers the common cases, names, contacts, addresses, text, numbers, dates, UUIDs, with unlimited files, no account, and everything local. If you need an API or exotic field types, Mockaroo is the honest upgrade; for quick test data, this is faster.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can the fake data be used for fraud?',
      a: 'No, and that is by design, not just by request. Names, addresses, and numbers are random combinations that do not correspond to real people, emails use reserved domains, and phone numbers use the fictional 555 exchange. It is test data for development, and nothing about it helps impersonate anyone.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <section class="tool-card" slot="tool" aria-label="Fake data generator">
    <h3 class="tool-step">Columns</h3>
    <div id="fakedata-col-list"></div>
    <div class="field-row">
      <select id="fakedata-add-type" aria-label="Column type to add"></select>
      <button id="fakedata-add-col" class="btn btn-secondary" type="button">Add column</button>
    </div>
    <div class="row-2">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="fakedata-rows">Rows</label>
        <select id="fakedata-rows" aria-label="Number of rows"></select>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label>Format</label>
        <div class="radio-row">
          <label class="checkline"><input type="radio" name="fakedata-format" value="csv" checked /> CSV</label>
          <label class="checkline"><input type="radio" name="fakedata-format" value="json" /> JSON</label>
          <label class="checkline"><input type="radio" name="fakedata-format" value="sql" /> SQL</label>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="field" id="fakedata-table-wrap" hidden>
      <label for="fakedata-table">SQL table name</label>
      <input type="text" id="fakedata-table" maxlength="64" autocomplete="off" />
    </div>
    <div class="field">
      <label for="fakedata-seed">Seed <span class="hint">same seed and settings always give the same file</span></label>
      <div class="seed-row">
        <input type="text" id="fakedata-seed" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" />
        <button id="fakedata-reroll" class="btn btn-secondary" type="button">Re-roll</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div id="fakedata-error" class="error-box" hidden></div>
    <h3 class="tool-step">Preview</h3>
    <div id="fakedata-preview"></div>
    <div id="fakedata-result" class="result" hidden>
      <h3>Ready</h3>
      <p id="fakedata-stats" class="count-label"></p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <button id="fakedata-download" class="btn" type="button">Download file</button>
        <button id="fakedata-copy" class="btn btn-secondary" type="button">Copy to clipboard</button>
        <button id="fakedata-reset" class="btn btn-secondary" type="button">Reset columns</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <script>
    import { initFakeDataGenerator } from '../tools/fake-data-generator';
    initFakeDataGenerator();
  </script>
</ToolPage>
